## PART 3 — WHAT BREAKS WHEN REVOCATION IS EXPIRY

### 3a. Let's Encrypt, March 2020 — the cleanest measured case of "we chose to let them expire"

Sources: https://community.letsencrypt.org/t/2020-02-29-caa-rechecking-bug/114591 and https://community.letsencrypt.org/t/revoking-certain-certificates-on-march-4/114864.

- **Bug introduced 2019-07-25.** Discovered **2020-02-29 03:08 UTC**; issuance halted **03:10 UTC**; fix deployed **05:22 UTC**.
- Nature: Boulder rechecks CAA within 8 hours before issuance. *"when a certificate request contained N domain names that needed CAA rechecking, Boulder would pick one domain name and check it N times."*
- **Scale: 3,048,289 affected certificates, ~2.6% of active certificates.** ~1M of those were duplicates covering the same domain sets.
- Compliance deadline: **2020-03-05 03:00 UTC** (2020-03-04 21:00 US ET).
- **Actually revoked: 1,706,505** certificates *"that we are confident were replaced during the incident period"*, plus **445** treated as highest priority (those with CAA records prohibiting LE issuance at discovery).
- **Deliberately NOT revoked: over 1 million** (of the ~3M, netting roughly 1.3M unrevoked after dedup), past the BR compliance deadline. Stated reasoning: revoking them would *"cause concern for their visitors"*, **and since certificates expire in 90 days the affected unrevoked certificates would leave circulation relatively quickly.**

**This is the design's thesis executed by a major CA under regulatory obligation, and it is the measured cost: ~1.3 million certificates known to be misissued were left valid, by choice, on the argument that expiry would clean up.** The BR §5.7.1.2 mass-revocation-plan mandate (effective 2025-12-01) is the ecosystem's direct answer to this class of event.

### 3b. DigiNotar 2011 — revocation could not do the job; distrust took ~11 days

Timeline (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DigiNotar):
- **2011-06-19**: DigiNotar first detects the intrusion.
- **2011-07-10**: attacker issues the wildcard `*.google.com` certificate.
- **2011-08-28/29**: certificate problems observed on Iranian ISPs; the fraudulent cert detected — **~49 days after issuance**.
- **2011-08-29**: Mozilla and Microsoft remove DigiNotar roots.
- **2011-08-30**: Opera initially states no update is needed *because it does CRL checking*; later revokes trust anyway.
- **2011-09-02**: Mozilla temporarily preserves certain PKIoverheid intermediates.
- **2011-09-03**: Dutch government assumes operational control.
- **2011-09-08**: Opera completes root blacklisting.
- **2011-09-09**: Apple ships Security Update 2011-005 for OS X; **iOS not patched until 2011-10-13** — **~46 days after detection**.
- **2011-09-20**: bankruptcy.
- Fox-IT investigation: **~531 misissued certificates**; **~300,000 Iranian Gmail users** identified as the main target (via OCSP responder logs).
- The decisive line: DigiNotar *"could not guarantee all such certificates had been revoked"* — revocation lists alone proved insufficient, and every browser had to ship a **binary update** to distrust the root.

Note the direct contradiction of the CA/B Forum's "expiry is reliable" framing on one axis and its confirmation on another: **Opera's position was that CRL checking sufficed, and it was wrong.** Meanwhile the fix that actually worked — pushing a root removal via the software update channel — is neither a lease nor a revocation; it is out-of-band code distribution.

### 3c. Expiry failing in the other direction — outages

- **Ericsson, 2018-12-06** (https://www.ericsson.com/en/press-releases/2018/12/update-on-software-issue-impacting-certain-customers): *"an expired certificate in specific software versions of the SGSN–MME"*, affecting *"a limited number of customers"* across *"multiple countries"* using **two specific software versions**. By end of day 2018-12-06 *"most of the affected customers' network services have been successfully restored."* CEO Börje Ekholm: *"The faulty software that has caused these issues is being decommissioned."* **Ericsson's own release gives no user counts.** The ~32M UK (O2/Telefónica) and SoftBank Japan figures come from press coverage (The Register 2018-12-06, MacRumors 2018-12-07, The Verge 2018-12-07 — the last host blocked to WebFetch); **I did not verify the 32M figure from a primary source.**
- **DST Root CA X3 expiry**: Let's Encrypt's mitigation was a **3-year cross-sign of ISRG Root X1 from DST Root CA X3 by IdenTrust**, announced **2020-12-21** (https://letsencrypt.org/2020/12/21/extending-android-compatibility.html), cross-sign set to expire **early 2024**. **The blog post I read gives no expiry date for DST Root CA X3 itself and no Android-share percentage** — those figures are **NOT REACHED**.
- **Microsoft Teams Feb 2020 expired-cert outage**: **NOT REACHED.**

### 3d. The offline problem — a node offline longer than its lease

**Certificate Transparency's detection floor.** RFC 6962 §3 (text read): the SCT is *"the log's promise to incorporate the certificate in the Merkle Tree within a fixed amount of time known as the Maximum Merge Delay (MMD)"*; *"The log MUST incorporate a certificate in its Merkle Tree within the Maximum Merge Delay period after the issuance of the SCT"*; *"A certificate accompanied by an SCT can be verified against any STH dated after the SCT timestamp + the Maximum Merge Delay."* **RFC 6962 itself specifies no numeric MMD** — it is per-log policy. **Chrome's CT log policy** (https://github.com/chromium/ct-policy/blob/main/log_policy.md) now caps it: *"static-ct-api logs must not specify a MMD greater than **1 minute**"*; *"RFC 6962 logs must not specify a MMD greater than **4 hour**"*; logs must *"Maintain log availability of 99% or above"* on a 90-day rolling average. (The commonly cited 24h MMD is the older figure; the current policy is tighter. Worth noting for a design that budgets detection latency.)

**Roughtime.** https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-ntp-roughtime/ — **still not an RFC.** Current state: **draft-ietf-ntp-roughtime-19, dated 2026-03-17**, intended status **Experimental**, *"In Progress (First Edit)"* at the RFC Editor. Purpose: *"secure rough time synchronization even for clients without any idea of what time it is."* Directly on the lease-validation problem: *"An application that needs to verify X.509 certificates… but lacks an accurate and trusted time source can use Roughtime to obtain a time estimate."* It also provides cryptographic **malfeasance reports** proving server misbehavior without a pre-existing accurate clock. **A design whose security rests on expiry rests on the client's clock, and the IETF's answer to that has been in draft for years and is Experimental.**

**DTN / BPSec — the prior art punts.** RFC 9172 (BPSec) **§6 Key Management**, quoted in full:
> *"There exists a myriad of ways to establish, communicate, and otherwise manage key information in DTN. Certain DTN deployments might follow established protocols for key management, whereas other DTN deployments might require new and novel approaches. **BPSec assumes that key management is handled as a separate part of network management; this specification neither defines nor requires a specific strategy for key management.**"*

The word "revocation" does not appear in RFC 9172's key-management section. RFC 9171 (BPv7) *does* build the lease model into the data plane — the **Lifetime** field, *"When a bundle's age exceeds its lifetime, bundle nodes need no longer retain or…"*, plus **lifetime overrides**: a node *"MAY impose a temporary overriding lifetime of shorter duration; such an overriding lifetime SHALL NOT replace the lifetime… while the bundle resides at that node."* Status report reason code **1 = "Lifetime expired"**. Security note in §2102–2108: very long lifetimes *"could constitute a denial-of-service attack"*, **and so could imposing lifetime overrides** — i.e. both directions of the lease dial are named as attack surfaces.

**Reading**: the one deployed protocol family designed from first principles for arbitrarily long disconnection **implements leases for data and explicitly refuses to solve credential revocation at all.**

**Kerberos in disconnected ops** and any **clock-skew-causes-expiry-failure** paper: **NOT REACHED.**

### 3e. Insider / offboarding time-to-deprovisioning

**NOT REACHED as measured data.** Crossref sweeps for deprovisioning/ex-employee-access measurement returned nothing on topic; HN Algolia returned nothing. Rivest 1998 gives the *design* argument for why short leases solve offboarding (the fired Alice cannot obtain a fresh certificate), but I found **no measurement of actual deprovisioning latency** from a source I could reach. The parallel agent was tasked with this and may have vendor-survey-grade material; **treat any such figure as vendor-survey reliability, not measurement.**

