# ADDENDUM — Part 2 verified in depth, with three corrections to my own figures

## CORRECTIONS TO WHAT I WROTE ABOVE

**1. Kerberos default lifetimes — I flagged these NOT REACHED; they are now sourced, and the commonly-repeated figure is wrong.**
- **MIT Kerberos `kdc.conf`**: `max_life` default **24 hours**; `max_renewable_life` default **0** — renewal is off by default. https://web.mit.edu/kerberos/krb5-latest/doc/admin/conf_files/kdc_conf.html
- **Active Directory Default Domain Policy**: *Maximum lifetime for user ticket* **10 hours** (recommended 4–10h; **0 means tickets never expire**); *Maximum lifetime for user ticket renewal* **7 days**.
- The widely-cited "MIT default is 10h" conflates AD's default with MIT's. **MIT's is 24h.**

**2. The "reset krbtgt twice" guidance — reached, and its structure matters.** Microsoft Learn, *AD Forest Recovery — Reset the krbtgt password* (ms.date 2025-05-21), verbatim:
> *"**You should perform this operation twice. You must wait 10 hours between password resets.** 10 hours are the default Maximum lifetime for user ticket and Maximum lifetime for service ticket policy settings… The password history value for the krbtgt account is 2… By resetting the password twice you effectively clear any old passwords from the history."*

**The only revocation Kerberos has is gated on waiting one full lease interval.** Revoke faster and you break live authentication. And Microsoft's own tooling for it, `New-KrbtgtKeys.ps1`, is **archived** (github.com/microsoftarchive/New-KrbtgtKeys.ps1, archived 2024-03-07), with a README stating it was *"maintained by a few dedicated engineers from Microsoft outside of their normal work assignments in their spare time."*

**3. The "krbtgt default rotation is 10 years" claim: NOT REACHED, and probably not a documented default at all.** No Microsoft doc states a rotation interval. What is documented is that **AD ships no rotation schedule whatsoever** — krbtgt changes only on domain functional-level upgrade or manual reset.

---

## THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT NEW FACT — headline question 4, answered exactly

**Microsoft's Storm-0558 signing key was issued April 2016, nominally expired April 2021, and remained honored until it was finally rotated in July 2023.**

Source: Microsoft's September 2023 technical post-mortem (MSRC URL now 301-redirects to the blog index — **original NOT REACHED**), recovered via Wiz's analysis: https://www.wiz.io/blog/key-takeaways-from-microsofts-latest-storm-0558-report

- Key captured in **a crash dump from a crash in April 2021**; the dump crossed from the isolated production network into the corporate debugging environment.
- Storm-0558 plausibly held it **"for over two years prior to being discovered in June 2023."**
- Microsoft could only *"partially account for all of this threat actor's activity… between April 2021 and May 2023"* — log retention ran out.
- Root validation defect: the Azure AD SDK *"did not include functionality to properly validate an authentication token's issuer ID"*, letting a **consumer** MSA key mint **enterprise** Azure AD tokens.
- Microsoft's own incident analysis (2023-07-14, https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2023/07/14/analysis-of-storm-0558-techniques-for-unauthorized-email-access/): **~25 organizations**; activity began **2023-05-15**, discovered **2023-06-16 — by the customer (US State Department), not by Microsoft**. Mitigations: 2023-06-26 OWA stops accepting the API's tokens; 2023-06-27 tokens from the acquired key blocked; **2023-06-29 all MSA signing keys valid at the time of the incident revoked**; 2023-07-03 consumer key usage blocked.

**One key served seven years and outlived its own stated expiry by 27 months.** This is the recursive hazard, measured: the lease had an expiry field and the field did nothing, because the thing that honors expiry was the same system the key belonged to.

**CSRB caveat, important**: the CSRB report was released **2024-04-02** (report dated 2024-03-20; ~20 organizations and experts consulted). Its headline finding, per Wikipedia's summary: *"Microsoft's security culture was inadequate and requires an overhaul,"* and Microsoft *"failed to detect the compromise of its cryptographic crown jewels on its own, relying instead on a customer."* Victims: State, Commerce, House of Representatives. **The PDF itself is 403 from cisa.gov, dhs.gov and media.defense.gov to both curl and WebFetch, and no full-text mirror was found. The commonly-quoted "60,000 State Department emails" and "automated key rotation paused since 2021" are therefore NOT VERIFIED from primary source. Do not attribute them to the CSRB.**

---

## THE MEASURED CASE AGAINST REVOCATION — the full numbers

**Heartbleed infrastructure strain (IMC 2014, beyond the replacement figures I gave):**
- Baseline **491 revocations/day (σ=101)** pre-disclosure.
- **GlobalSign revoked 56,353 certificates in two days — 50.2% of their visible certificates** (CloudFlare revoking all customer certs).
- **GoDaddy revoked 243,823 certificates** in week-long bursts over three months.
- **In the three months after disclosure, roughly as many revocations were processed as in the previous three years.**
- **GlobalSign's CRL grew from 2 KB to 4.7 MB.**
- CloudFlare's estimated revocation cost: **+40 Gbps sustained, ≈ $400,000/month.** (Langley's separate 2014-04-29 figure: Cloudflare's CRL serving at **"half a million dollars a month"**; all available revocations ≈ **39 MB**, projected to **~35 million revocations ≈ 300 MB raw** within five years — https://www.imperialviolet.org/2014/04/29/revocationagain.html)
- Vulnerability: lower-bound **23.7%** of Alexa Top 1M HTTPS sites at disclosure; **3% still vulnerable ~2 months later**; 56% of vulnerable hosts were embedded devices.

**Liu et al. IMC 2015 — the coverage arithmetic:**
- **11,461,935 revoked certificates observed across crawled CRLs; only 41,105 ever appeared in a CRLSet.** That is the 0.35%.
- CRLSets covered **62 of 1,584 distinct CA public keys (3.9%)**; only **295 of 2,800 CRLs (10.5%)** ever had any entry.
- Popularity does not help: Alexa Top 1M — **42,225 revocations in CRLs, 1,644 (3.9%) in CRLSets**. Top 1,000 — **392 revoked, 41 (10.4%)**.
- CRLSet peaked at **~25,000 entries during Heartbleed**, then shrank by more than a third.
- Weighted CRL size: **median certificate's CRL is 51 KB; largest observed 76 MB** (Apple).
- Browser matrix: **244 certificate configurations × 30 browser/OS combinations. Not a single mobile browser checked revocation for any certificate.** Chrome 44 on OS X did no revocation check at all for non-EV. Three revocation-checking bugs found and filed.
- Verdict, verbatim: *"our results paint a bleak picture of the ability to effectively revoke certificates today."*

**Let's Encrypt OCSP scale at shutdown** (https://letsencrypt.org/2025/08/06/ocsp-service-has-reached-end-of-life/): **~340 billion OCSP requests per month; >140,000 req/s at the CDN, 15,000 req/s at origin.** OCSP URLs had been out of certificates for >90 days before the responders went dark, so no unexpired certificate still pointed at one.

---

## THE LEASE SIDE — what actually shipped, and what it kept

**SC-081v3 precise dates**: discussion opened **2025-01-28 17:00 UTC**, closed **2025-04-01 17:00 UTC**; voting **2025-04-04 19:30 UTC → 2025-04-11 19:30 UTC**; ballot created in the Server Certificate WG **2025-04-08**. **Proposed by Clint Wilson (Apple)**, endorsed by Nick France (Sectigo), Ryan Dickson (Google Chrome), Ben Wilson (Mozilla). The SHOULD-NOT values sit one day below each MUST-NOT: **397 / 199 / 99 / 46**. "For the purpose of calculations, a day is measured as 86,400 seconds." Prior art the ballot cites: **Ballot 185** (2017-02-24) and **SC-22** (2019-09-10); **SC-063** created a Short-Lived Subscriber Certificate class at **10 days, reducing to 7**. Ballot footnote [6]: *"Fundamentally, the above property of certificates is not something which can be addressed through revocation of certificates without much more substantial changes to the ecosystem."* Evidence cited: https://insecure.design/ and Ma et al., stale certificates, IMC 2023 (https://zanema.com/papers/imc23_stale_certs.pdf).

**Let's Encrypt short-lived certs — plan vs delivery (the plan slipped by a year):**

| Announced (2025-01-16) | Actually happened |
|---|---|
| first self-issued Feb 2025 | **2025-02-20** — first short-lived cert issued |
| early adopters ~April 2025 | (IP certs: **2025-07-01** first issued) |
| **GA end of 2025** | **GA 2026-01-15** |

GA details (https://letsencrypt.org/2026/01/15/6day-and-ip-general-availability/): profile name **`shortlived`**, lifetime **160 hours**, IPv4 and IPv6, IP certs *must* be short-lived, **opt-in not default**. **Six-day certs carry no OCSP or CRL URLs at all** — the only pure expiry-as-revocation artifact in the file.

**And the default is moving too** (https://letsencrypt.org/2025/12/02/from-90-to-45/): **2026-05-13** opt-in `tlsserver` profile → 45 days; **2027-02-10** default `classic` → **64 days** with a **10-day** authorization reuse period; **2028-02-16** `classic` → **45 days** with a **7-hour** authorization reuse period. Founding rationale (2015-11-09, https://letsencrypt.org/2015/11/09/why-90-days/): 90 days chosen to limit compromise damage and force automation; **Firefox telemetry then showed 29% of TLS transactions on 90-day certs, more than any other lifetime.**

---

## MACAROONS — a negative finding worth stating plainly

Birgisson, Politz, Erlingsson, Taly, Vrable, Lentczner, NDSS 2014. PDF: https://storage.googleapis.com/gweb-research2023-media/pubtools/pdf/41892.pdf

Model: **nested chained HMACs**; any holder can *attenuate* offline by appending a caveat — no issuer round-trip, no PKI. **First-party caveats** are context predicates (`time < 5/1/13 3pm`, source IP, ChannelID, resource prefix) — expiry is just one of these. **Third-party caveats** require a discharge macaroon from a named party; the paper's own enumerated uses include a network time authority, identity providers, auditing, and **a third-party revocation-checking service**.

Its §II states the design's thesis directly: the four strategies are *"(i) using very short-lived credentials, (ii) allowing the addition of freshness constraints, (iii) relying on external, authoritative state (such as revocation lists, or epoch counters), and (iv) splitting credentials… **Macaroons excel at all four** … due to their efficiency and flexibility, **macaroons may live only for seconds**."*

**But: the paper describes only prototypes** — Python 2.7.2 and JavaScript, measured in Chrome v27 with the Stanford JS Crypto library. **It never claims a Google production deployment, and no Google source was found that does.** The premise in the brief ("what did Google actually deploy it in") appears to have no supporting evidence.

**Where macaroons actually run:**
- **Fly.io — production, documented** (https://fly.io/blog/macaroons-escalated-quickly/, HN 2024-01-31, 263 pts; https://fly.io/blog/operationalizing-macaroons/, 2025-03-28). API tokens, per-app/machine/volume scoping, SSO, service-to-service auth. They use a `ValidityWindow` caveat for expiry **and they built explicit revocation-by-nonce** with a central verification service holding a revocation list, noting revocation "is rare." **Expiry primary, revocation retained.**
- **Canonical**: `snapd` (59 code hits), `candid` identity service (117 hits), `juju/juju` (302 hits), `go-macaroon-bakery/macaroon-bakery`.
- **Storj** (https://storj.io/blog/2019/05/flexible-file-sharing-with-macaroons/, 2019-05).
- **HashiCorp: NOT REACHED** — no evidence found; treat any such claim as unverified.
- Practitioner mood datapoint: HN "Ask HN: Are Macaroons widely used in the industry for Authorization" (2019-10-22) drew ~1 point.

**Biscuit**: biscuitsec.org **403'd**. From the spec repo (https://github.com/biscuit-auth/biscuit): latest spec **v3.3, 2024-12-17**; 1,147 stars; Rust `biscuit-auth` **6.0.0, 2025-07-16**; the project has **moved under the Eclipse Foundation** (`eclipse-biscuit/*`). Full v3.x support only in Rust/WASM/Python/Haskell; **Go and .NET are stuck at v1/v3.0**. Expiry is a Datalog check: `check if time($0), $0 < 2019-02-05T23:00:00Z`. **Revocation is supported but delegated to the host**: the authorizer emits `revocation_id(<block index>, <byte array>)` facts keyed on the block *signature*, and the host supplies the list. Named deployment: **Apache Pulsar at Clever Cloud** (github.com/clevercloud/biscuit-pulsar). CVE-2022-31053 affected biscuit-auth ≥1.0.0 <2.0.0 and biscuit-go <2.0. **No other named large deployer found.**

---

## SPIFFE — the doctrinal statement I could not find, found

It is not on spiffe.io's concepts page; it is in the official book, *Solving the Bottom Turtle* (https://spiffe.io/pdf/Solving-the-bottom-turtle-SPIFFE-SPIRE-Book.pdf). First the problem: *"In practice, most software will fail open, continuing to trust certificates when CRLs are unavailable."* Then the doctrine, verbatim:

> *"One compromise in solving the challenges posed by revocation is to rely more heavily on expiration. If the certificate lifetime is very short (perhaps only a few hours), then the CA can frequently re-perform any checks that it originally made. **If certificates renew frequently enough, then CRLs might not even be necessary since it may be faster just to wait for the certificate to expire.**"*

The book is candid that this is a dial, not a constant: *"there's no 'magic' TTL that is a best choice for all deployments"*; TTL must exceed document-delivery latency; SPIRE agents warn when renewal takes longer than half the TTL.

**And SPIFFE keeps revocation hooks anyway.** `SPIFFE_Workload_API.md` streams *"revocations and CA certificate introductions"*; both `X509SVIDResponse` and `X509BundlesResponse` carry optional `crl` fields. The newer `WIT-SVID.md` is blunt: the `jti` *"could be leveraged for revocation of an individual WIT-SVID. **There are no mechanisms defined within SPIFFE for the propagation of WIT-SVID revocations and this is considered out of the scope of the specification.**"* Its `exp` *"is the primary control of the length of time for which a WIT-SVID is valid"*, typically **"minutes to hours"**, with validators permitted to reject SVIDs whose `exp − nbf` exceeds administrative policy.

(TTL defaults confirmed independently by both of us: `default_x509_svid_ttl` **1h**, `default_jwt_svid_ttl` **5m**; `agent_ttl` inherits the x509 value. Note the sample config in the docs shows `6h`, so 1h is the code default, not what a reader copying the sample gets.)

---

## DNS TTL — the lease honored, the lease overridden

Moura, Heidemann, Schmidt, Hardaker, **"Cache Me If You Can: Effects of DNS Time-to-Live"**, IMC 2019, DOI 10.1145/3355369.3355568, published **2019-10-21**. https://ant.isi.edu/~johnh/PAPERS/Moura19b.pdf

All MEASURED (RIPE Atlas + authoritative-side traffic):
- **Short TTLs are mostly honored**: *"such manipulation is very rare for TTLs shorter than 1h, at least as seen from RIPE Atlas VPs"* (~190k and ~280k valid responses).
- **Long TTLs are silently capped**: for `google.co` across **16,000 vantage points**, **~15% of answers — many via Google Public DNS — returned exactly 21,599 s, a 6-hour cap**; ~9% returned exactly 900 s (parent glue).
- **Which TTL governs is not deterministic. Only 52% (`.nl`, authoritative-side) to 90% (`.uy`, RIPE Atlas) of queries are child-centric — meaning 10–48% obey the *parent's* TTL**, which the zone owner often does not control.
- **Sticky resolvers**: after both NS and A records expired at t=120 min, **305–390 VPs (~2.25%) kept using the old server**.
- **The named incident**: in a July 2019 renumbering experiment, **13 of 161 responses returned the pre-renumber answer after TTL expiry**, all through **OpenDNS**. Diagnosed from pcap: OpenDNS is parent-centric and trusted the `.com` zone's **2-day NS TTL** for `zurrundeddu.com`, never refreshing the A record. Confirmed by control — with the child authoritatives offline, OpenDNS VPs still resolved while others got timeouts or SERVFAIL. **A 2-day parent lease silently overrode the operator's short child lease.**
- The counter-pressure: longer TTLs took median resolution from **183 ms → 28.7 ms** and cut authoritative traffic ~77%.
- Schomp et al., IMC 2013 (the source Moura cites for resolver TTL manipulation): **NOT REACHED** — both canonical PDF URLs 404.

---

## OKTA — the detail that reframes the incident

Okta's own root-cause post, 2023-11-03 (https://sec.okta.com/articles/2023/11/unauthorized-access-oktas-support-case-management-system-root-cause/):
- **Unauthorized access window: 2023-09-28 → 2023-10-17.**
- Root cause, verbatim: *"an employee had signed-in to their personal Google profile on the Chrome browser of their Okta-managed laptop. **The username and password of the service account had been saved into the employee's personal Google account.**"*
- **134 customers affected (<1% of the customer base); 5 experienced actual session hijacking.**
- Detection lag: **Sept 29** 1Password reports; **Oct 2** BeyondTrust reports; **Oct 13** BeyondTrust supplies an attacker IP; **Oct 16** Okta correlates it; **Oct 17** account disabled. **14 days of downloads went unseen** because different access methods generated different log event types.
- **Okta's mitigation was "revocation of embedded session tokens"** — an explicit fallback to revocation *because the tokens' own TTL was too long to save them*.
- Post-incident controls (https://sec.okta.com/harfiles): zero standing privileges, MFA for protected actions, **IP binding to prevent session takeover**, network-zone restriction on API access. **IP binding is a first-party caveat in macaroon terms — contextual confinement added precisely because expiry alone failed.**

Cloudflare figures independently confirmed by both of us. The agent's framing of the arithmetic is worth keeping: **4 missed credentials out of ~5,000 is ~0.08%, and it was sufficient.** Revocation-by-rotation is a hand enumeration, and per the standing lesson in this file, **a hand-written enumeration cannot notice what it missed.**

---

## REVISED HEADLINE ANSWERS

**1. Unification.** Unchanged: **no**, not under expiry. Matrix unifies the three under lineage-with-tiebreak and has broken at that seam twice (Synapse #8629 open since 2020; CVE-2025-49090 / CVE-2025-54315, fixed 2025-08-14 only by minting room version 12 + State Resolution 2.1). Keyhive attempts it under convergent capabilities, pre-alpha and unaudited, with its own authors naming concurrent mutual revocation as unresolved and cross-fork attacks as having *"no established security definition in prior decentralized schemes."* DCGKA factorizes rather than unifies — §5.2 assumes a DGM oracle. MLS unifies revocation+membership and discards revision, and §14 requires the application to supply a serializer.

**2. Convergence.** **Yes, and it is now dated policy** — 398→200→100→**47 days by 2029-03-15**, DCV reuse 398→**10 days**, voted 25-0-5 / 4-0-0 on 2025-04-11; Let's Encrypt OCSP dead 2025-08-06 after ~340 B requests/month; 160-hour certificates GA 2026-01-15 carrying **no revocation URLs at all**. **But every system in this file that adopted expiry kept revocation as a backstop** — SPIFFE keeps `crl` fields while declaring WIT-SVID revocation propagation out of scope; Biscuit emits `revocation_id` and hands the list to the host; Fly.io runs a validity window *and* revocation-by-nonce; Let's Encrypt still publishes CRLs; and the CA/B Forum made **annually-tested mass-revocation plans mandatory** (BR §5.7.1.2, effective 2025-12-01) in the same cycle. **The convergence is expiry-primary, not expiry-only. No reachable system has actually deleted revocation.**

**3. Measured cost of the convergence.** Let's Encrypt **knowingly left ~1.3 M misissued certificates unrevoked in March 2020** on the argument that 90-day expiry would clear them. Heartbleed: **4% of Alexa Top 1M revoked**, 19% of vulnerable replacers revoked, **14% reused the compromised key**. Liu 2015: **>8% of served certs revoked, 0.35% of revocations reaching CRLSets, zero mobile browsers checking**. DigiNotar: **~49 days from rogue issuance to detection, ~46 more to an iOS patch**, and the fix was a binary root removal, not a revocation.

**4. The recursive hazard — confirmed, three ways, with the strongest evidence now in hand.** Storm-0558: a signing key **issued April 2016, nominally expired April 2021, honored until rotated July 2023** — 27 months past its own expiry, discovered by a customer. Kerberos: the krbtgt hash lets an attacker **set arbitrary ticket lifetimes**, survives every user password reset, has **no default rotation schedule**, and its only cure requires **waiting one full lease between two resets** using tooling Microsoft has archived. Cloudflare: **0.08% of a rotation sweep missed → a 10-day intrusion across 76 repositories.**

**The pattern across all three: the lease held; the minter did not.** A design in which "a contract performs the key agreement" makes the contract the minter, and nothing in the reachable literature bounds how short the contract's own lease can be. BeeKEM's published answer is the **κ dial** — more retained secrets means better partition tolerance and weaker forward secrecy — offered with the explicit admission that **no impossibility result exists to say where the dial must stop.**

**The one instrument that could decide the claim** remains Kleppmann & Howard's **Theorem 3.1** (arXiv:2012.00472): BEC is achievable **iff** transactions are I-confluent with respect to the invariants. A hard "removed members must never read" invariant is not I-confluent; a monotone lease bound is. That is the design's strongest available justification — **and no source makes the argument, so it stands unreviewed.**

## STILL NOT REACHED

McDaniel & Rubin's rebuttal to Rivest (Springer auth wall; abstract publisher-elided; two author-host guesses failed) — **the most directly adversarial source to the thesis, metadata only**. The CSRB PDF (403 from three government hosts; the 60,000-emails and paused-rotation figures **unverified**). Microsoft's own September 2023 Storm-0558 post-mortem (301-redirected away). Jacob et al. SACMAT 2020 abstract (ACM DL 403). Schomp et al. IMC 2013. Ceramic streams; Automerge's own access-control position; git as a cited source. Measured offboarding/deprovisioning latency — **nothing found in Crossref or HN; treat any figure as vendor-survey grade**. DST Root CA X3's expiry date and Android-affected share; Teams Feb 2020; the O2 32M-user figure from a primary source (Ericsson's own release gives no counts). biscuitsec.org (403). OpenAlex exhausted its quota mid-session for both of us; Crossref carried the remainder.
